#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""autoharn-service -- ensure-running control plane (design/FABLE-AUTOHARN-UMBRELLA-CLI-SPEC.md
§2, ledger rows 1151-1183). Invoked as `autoharn service status|start|stop|restart` by the
./autoharn dispatcher (never invoked directly by a caller -- there is no `./service` alias, this
is not one of the ten relocated verbs).

RESTART (design/FABLE-SERVICE-DRAIN-RESTART-SPEC.md, row 1553 option A): a drained, witnessed hub
handover -- own-process check (verbatim `stop` posture), config pre-validation BEFORE any signal,
bounded SIGTERM drain (--drain-timeout, --force-kill), spawn via the SAME `ensure_running.
spawn_and_wait` `start` uses, a STRICTER handover contract than `start`'s adopt-is-success (a
foreign winner is REFUSED, never adopted), then a per-deployment /health witness before the one-
line summary. See `cmd_restart` below for the leg-by-leg implementation; `serving/
boundary_service.py` itself is NOT modified by this verb (the spec's own §2 non-goal).

WHAT THIS DOES, per the spec's own words:
  - PROBE: every invocation that needs the boundary resolves it -- reachable, use it, regardless
    of who started it. "Reachable" means a version-COMPATIBLE autoharn boundary answers its own
    /d/{boundary_deployment}/health shape -- not merely "some HTTP server responded" (round-1
    review finding SEVERE-4: any 200/404/etc used to count as reachable, which would silently
    ADOPT a squatting non-autoharn process). A squatter or a version-skewed peer refuses loudly
    by name; neither is ever adopted nor spawned over.
  - SPAWN-AS-DETACHED-CHILD: unreachable -> consult this world's own deployment record and
    boundary-multiplex.toml, spawn `python3 -m serving.boundary_service` as an ordinary detached
    child process (plain subprocess, its own log file under this world's own directory; no
    systemd, no D-Bus -- row 1154), print ONE line saying so, then proceed.
  - BIND-AS-LOCK RACE, STRUCTURAL (round-1 review finding SEVERE-2): the port bind IS the lock,
    and ownership of the win is now decided STRUCTURALLY, not by a timing grace-sleep. Every
    spawned child is launched with `--pidfile <this world's pidfile path>`; `serving/
    boundary_service.py`'s own `--pidfile` handling binds its OWN listen socket itself,
    synchronously, BEFORE uvicorn/ASGI ever starts, and writes the pidfile immediately after that
    bind call succeeds (deliberately NOT an ASGI 'startup' event hook -- the installed uvicorn
    runs that event BEFORE its own socket bind, which would make it fire for a losing racer too;
    see that module's own `--pidfile` help text for why). So: the pidfile appearing, naming OUR
    OWN child's pid, is structural proof this invocation's spawn won; the pidfile naming a
    DIFFERENT pid (or our own child exiting without ever writing it) is structural proof it lost
    -- ADOPT, per row 1165 ("someone-else-started-it is a success case"). No sleep-then-trust-
    poll() anywhere in this file.
  - LOUD REFUSAL: a service that genuinely cannot start (the port is held by something that does
    NOT answer this deployment's own /health shape, or answers it with an incompatible protocol
    version, or the config is invalid) refuses loudly with the diagnosis -- never a silent hang,
    never a guessed alternate port, never an adopt of a non-autoharn responder.
  - EXPLICIT STOP: `autoharn service stop` never fights a start ensure-running performed within
    the SAME invocation's scope (there is no such overlap here -- stop is its own separate
    invocation) but DOES refuse, rather than silently no-op, when this world has no record of
    having started the service itself (no pidfile) -- killing a process this tool never started
    is exactly the kind of action-at-a-distance ADR-0002 forbids. It also refuses (round-1 review
    finding SEVERE-3) when the pidfile's own pid is no longer a `serving.boundary_service`
    process at all (checked via `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`) -- PID REUSE means a stale pidfile can
    name an unrelated, unlucky process; signaling it on trust would kill a bystander while
    reporting success. The stale pidfile is removed either way (it no longer describes anything
    this tool should trust), but nothing is ever signaled without the identity check passing.

PIDFILE: `<world-dir>/.autoharn-service.pid`, written ONLY by the spawned child ITSELF,
immediately after its own listen-socket bind succeeds (never by this script's parent process, and
never by an adopt) -- the one piece of state `stop` needs to act only on what it started, and the
one piece of state `start`'s own race resolution reads back to tell "mine" from "someone else's".
Absence is honest, not an error: an operator-started or hand-launched service has no pidfile, and
`stop` says so rather than guessing a pid.

Lazy imports are banned (CLAUDE.md, 2026-07-02): every import is top-of-file. No bare types in
new Python (ledger row 1105): every function signature below carries its return-type annotation.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import json
import os
import signal
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path
from urllib.parse import urlparse

HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent          # libexec/autoharn/ is the SIBLING dir this file
                                                  # lives beside (libexec/autoharn-service is
                                                  # itself directly under libexec/, one level up
                                                  # from libexec/autoharn/); AUTOHARN_ROOT climbs
                                                  # one parent from THIS file's own directory.
AUTOHARN_ROOT = HERE.parent
DEPLOYMENT_PATH = Path(os.environ.get("PICKUP_DEPLOYMENT", str(AUTOHARN_ROOT / "deployment.json")))

# serving/ensure_running.py is THE shared home for probe/spawn/race-resolution logic (round-1
# review DIRECTED WORK: factored out so BOTH this explicit control-plane verb AND every served
# CLI shim's own ensure-running-on-demand policy call the SAME structurally-correct code, ADR-0012
# P1 -- one home, not two drifting copies). serving/boundary_cli_client.py remains the home for
# "how does a caller talk to the boundary and classify what came back"
# (BoundaryUnreachable/BoundaryRefusal/ProtocolVersionMismatch); ensure_running.py imports it.
sys.path.insert(0, str(AUTOHARN_ROOT))
from serving import ensure_running as er  # noqa: E402
# design/FABLE-SERVICE-DRAIN-RESTART-SPEC.md §1 leg 2: serving/boundary_multiplex_config.py is
# THE ONE shape authority for boundary-multiplex.toml (ADR-0012 P1) -- `restart` imports it
# rather than re-deriving or re-parsing the TOML's own shape a second time here.
from serving import boundary_multiplex_config as bmc  # noqa: E402
sys.path.insert(0, str(AUTOHARN_ROOT / "filing"))
import fixture_sandbox  # noqa: E402  (filing/fixture_sandbox.py, design/FABLE-FIXTURE-SANDBOX-
# RUNTIME-FORECLOSURE-SPEC.md §2 -- checked here too, defense-in-depth against a fixture that
# invokes this file directly instead of `autoharn service ...`, which ./autoharn's own dispatcher
# already checks before ever reaching this file)


def _load_deployment() -> dict:
    if not DEPLOYMENT_PATH.is_file():
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- no deployment record at {DEPLOYMENT_PATH}. "
              f"There is nothing to ensure-running without a deployment.json naming "
              f"boundary_url/boundary_deployment. Nothing was touched.", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)
    try:
        return json.loads(DEPLOYMENT_PATH.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError) as e:
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- {DEPLOYMENT_PATH} could not be read as JSON "
              f"({e.__class__.__name__}: {e}). Nothing was touched.", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)


def _boundary_url_and_port(dep: dict) -> tuple[str, int]:
    url = dep.get("boundary_url")
    if not url:
        print("autoharn service: REFUSED -- this deployment.json has no 'boundary_url' -- "
              "there is no boundary for this world to ensure-running (design/"
              "FABLE-BOUNDARY-MULTIPLEX-AND-CLI-REBASE-SPEC.md §5: both boundary_url and "
              "boundary_deployment are refused-if-absent, never guessed). Run the setup wizard "
              "against this world to configure one. Nothing was touched.", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)
    parsed = urlparse(url)
    if not parsed.port:
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- boundary_url {url!r} names no port. Nothing was "
              f"touched.", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)
    return url, parsed.port


def _boundary_deployment(dep: dict) -> str:
    name = dep.get("boundary_deployment")
    if not name:
        print("autoharn service: REFUSED -- this deployment.json has no 'boundary_deployment' -- "
              "ensure-running cannot probe this world's own /health shape without it (design/"
              "FABLE-BOUNDARY-MULTIPLEX-AND-CLI-REBASE-SPEC.md §5: both boundary_url and "
              "boundary_deployment are refused-if-absent, never guessed). Nothing was touched.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)
    return name


def _pidfile_path() -> Path:
    return DEPLOYMENT_PATH.parent / ".autoharn-service.pid"


# ROW 1332 FIX: stop used to send SIGTERM and remove the pidfile unconditionally, with no wait
# and no exit confirmation -- a slow-to-die boundary_service was orphaned (still running, still
# holding the port) while the pidfile's disappearance made the world LOOK stopped. These three
# constants and _wait_for_pid_exit below give stop a wait-with-timeout-then-escalate posture,
# mirroring the shape seen-red/setup-tui-config-file/run_fixtures.py's own `_kill_boundary`
# already adopted for exactly this same class of teardown (rows 1330/1331 arc) -- a graceful
# SIGTERM first, confirmed via /proc/<pid> liveness polling (this pid is essentially never this
# process's own child -- stop runs as a separate invocation from whichever start spawned it, so
# os.waitpid is not available; /proc existence is the only trustworthy, non-blocking liveness
# signal here), escalating to SIGKILL only if still alive after the grace window, and refusing to
# remove the pidfile if the process is STILL alive after that (uninterruptible kernel sleep is
# the only realistic way SIGKILL fails to end a process promptly, but "realistic" is not
# "impossible" -- reporting success while a live pid is orphaned is exactly the defect this fixes).
# PID_REUSE, second-signal case (review 2026-07-26): pid identity (pid_is_boundary_service) is
# verified ONCE, before the first SIGTERM -- it is deliberately NOT re-verified before the SIGKILL
# escalation, so a pid recycled to an unrelated process inside the 5s grace window would be
# SIGKILL'd blind. Accepted, matching the row-1340 _kill_boundary precedent's identical posture;
# the window is seconds and the pid was boundary-shaped at entry. Disclosed, not silently absent.
_STOP_SIGTERM_TIMEOUT_S = 5.0   # grace period for a clean shutdown; matches _kill_boundary's own
                                # 5.0s SIGTERM grace window above, chosen there (and here) as
                                # generous enough for an ordinary ASGI app's shutdown handlers to
                                # run without making an operator's `stop` feel hung.
_STOP_SIGKILL_TIMEOUT_S = 2.0   # confirmation window after SIGKILL -- SIGKILL is uncatchable and
                                # unblockable, so a healthy process ends near-instantly; this is a
                                # short confirmation poll, not a second grace period.
_STOP_POLL_INTERVAL_S = 0.1     # poll cadence for both windows above -- frequent enough that the
                                # common case (process dies well within its window) resolves fast,
                                # coarse enough not to hammer /proc.


def _wait_for_pid_exit(pid: int, timeout_s: float, poll_interval_s: float) -> bool:
    """Poll /proc/<pid> until it disappears (process exited) or timeout_s elapses. Returns True
    the moment exit is observed, False if the deadline passes with the pid still present. No
    os.waitpid anywhere -- see the constants' comment above for why this pid is not treated as
    this process's own child."""
    deadline = time.monotonic() + timeout_s
    while time.monotonic() < deadline:
        if not os.path.exists(f"/proc/{pid}"):
            return True
        time.sleep(poll_interval_s)
    return not os.path.exists(f"/proc/{pid}")


def cmd_status(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    dep = _load_deployment()
    url, port = _boundary_url_and_port(dep)
    boundary_deployment = _boundary_deployment(dep)
    outcome, detail = er.probe(url, boundary_deployment)
    if outcome == er.OK:
        print(f"autoharn service: REACHABLE at {url} (port {port}, deployment "
              f"{boundary_deployment!r}, protocol-compatible).")
        return 0
    if outcome == er.REFUSED:
        print(f"autoharn service: {detail}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    print(f"autoharn service: UNREACHABLE at {url} (port {port}). Run 'autoharn service start' "
          f"to spawn it, or 'autoharn <verb>' will ensure-run it automatically.", file=sys.stderr)
    return 1


def cmd_start(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    dep = _load_deployment()
    url, port = _boundary_url_and_port(dep)
    boundary_deployment = _boundary_deployment(dep)
    outcome, detail = er.probe(url, boundary_deployment)
    if outcome == er.REFUSED:
        print(f"autoharn service: {detail}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if outcome == er.OK:
        print(f"autoharn service: already running at {url} -- nothing to do.")
        return 0
    world_dir = DEPLOYMENT_PATH.parent
    print(f"autoharn: service not running at {url} -- spawning it.", file=sys.stderr)
    result = er.spawn_and_wait(world_dir, url, port, boundary_deployment)
    if result.status == "own":
        print(f"autoharn service: reachable at {url} (this invocation's own spawn, "
              f"pid {result.proc_pid}, logs at {result.log_path}).")
        return 0
    if result.status == "adopted":
        if result.winner_pid is not None:
            print(f"autoharn service: reachable at {url} -- this invocation's own spawn "
                  f"(pid {result.proc_pid}) lost the bind race to a concurrent invocation's spawn "
                  f"(pid {result.winner_pid}) and is exiting (see {result.log_path}). ADOPTING "
                  f"the winner (row 1165: someone-else-started-it is a success case). No pidfile "
                  f"written by this invocation -- it does not own the winning process.")
        else:
            print(f"autoharn service: reachable at {url} -- this invocation's own spawn "
                  f"(pid {result.proc_pid}) lost the bind race and exited (see {result.log_path}); "
                  f"ADOPTING whichever process won the port (row 1165: someone-else-"
                  f"started-it is a success case). No pidfile written -- this invocation "
                  f"does not own the winning process.")
        return 0
    print(f"autoharn service: {result.detail}", file=sys.stderr)
    return 1


def cmd_stop(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    dep = _load_deployment()
    url, port = _boundary_url_and_port(dep)
    boundary_deployment = _boundary_deployment(dep)
    world_dir = DEPLOYMENT_PATH.parent
    toml_path = world_dir / "boundary-multiplex.toml"
    pidfile = _pidfile_path()
    if not pidfile.is_file():
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- no pidfile at {pidfile}. This tool has no record "
              f"of having started the service at {url} itself (it may be running under an "
              f"operator's own manual start, or under a different tool) -- stopping a process "
              f"this tool never started is refused rather than guessed. Stop it by hand if you "
              f"started it that way.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    try:
        pid = int(pidfile.read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip())
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- {pidfile} does not contain a readable pid "
              f"({e.__class__.__name__}: {e}). Nothing was touched.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if not er.pid_is_boundary_service(pid, toml_path):
        pidfile.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        # ROUND-4 REVIEW SEVERE-B FIX (item 3): the pidfile pid failing this identity check
        # (dead, unparseable, or an unrelated process -- PID REUSE can produce any of these) does
        # NOT mean nothing is serving at `url` right now -- the stale-pidfile-squat trap this
        # round's review named means a REAL, UNRECORDED winner can be answering this exact URL at
        # this exact moment while this pidfile named something else entirely. The pre-fix message
        # here was clean-success-shaped ("stale pidfile was removed; nothing was signaled") with
        # no hint that the operator's actual boundary might still be up and simply untracked --
        # probe it, and if it answers, TEACH the recovery instead of leaving the operator to
        # rediscover it by hand.
        unrecorded_note = ""
        outcome, _detail = er.probe(url, boundary_deployment)
        if outcome == er.OK:
            unrecorded_note = (
                f" NOTE: {url} still answers as a healthy, protocol-compatible boundary right "
                f"now -- this looks like an UNRECORDED winner (e.g. a stale pidfile squat this "
                f"tool's own spawn lost to, or an operator-started process this tool never "
                f"tracked). This tool has no record of that pid and will not guess it. Check "
                f"{world_dir / 'service.log'} for which pid actually bound the port, or run `ss "
                f"-ltnp | grep :{port}` directly to find it, then stop that pid by hand.")
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- pidfile named pid {pid}, which is not a "
              f"serving.boundary_service process for this world's own boundary-multiplex.toml "
              f"({toml_path}) -- PID REUSE can make a stale pidfile name an unrelated process; "
              f"signaling it on trust risks killing a bystander while reporting success. The "
              f"stale pidfile was removed; nothing was signaled.{unrecorded_note}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    try:
        os.kill(pid, signal.SIGTERM)
    except ProcessLookupError:
        print(f"autoharn service: pid {pid} (from {pidfile}) is already gone -- removing the "
              f"stale pidfile.", file=sys.stderr)
        pidfile.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        return 0
    except OSError as e:
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- could not signal pid {pid} ({e.__class__.__name__}: "
              f"{e}). Nothing was touched.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if _wait_for_pid_exit(pid, _STOP_SIGTERM_TIMEOUT_S, _STOP_POLL_INTERVAL_S):
        pidfile.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        print(f"autoharn service: pid {pid} (was serving {url}) confirmed dead after SIGTERM; "
              f"pidfile removed.")
        return 0
    print(f"autoharn service: pid {pid} (was serving {url}) did NOT exit within "
          f"{_STOP_SIGTERM_TIMEOUT_S}s of SIGTERM -- ESCALATING to SIGKILL.", file=sys.stderr)
    try:
        os.kill(pid, signal.SIGKILL)
    except ProcessLookupError:
        pidfile.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        print(f"autoharn service: pid {pid} exited between the timeout check and SIGKILL; "
              f"pidfile removed.")
        return 0
    except OSError as e:
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- SIGTERM timed out and SIGKILL could not be sent to "
              f"pid {pid} ({e.__class__.__name__}: {e}). The pidfile was NOT removed -- pid {pid} "
              f"may still be alive.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if _wait_for_pid_exit(pid, _STOP_SIGKILL_TIMEOUT_S, _STOP_POLL_INTERVAL_S):
        pidfile.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        print(f"autoharn service: pid {pid} (was serving {url}) confirmed dead after SIGKILL "
              f"(SIGTERM alone was not enough within {_STOP_SIGTERM_TIMEOUT_S}s); pidfile "
              f"removed.")
        return 0
    print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- pid {pid} is STILL ALIVE {_STOP_SIGKILL_TIMEOUT_S}s "
          f"after SIGKILL (likely stuck in uninterruptible kernel sleep) -- the pidfile was NOT "
          f"removed, because removing it now would orphan a demonstrably-live process while "
          f"reporting success. Investigate pid {pid} by hand.", file=sys.stderr)
    return 1


# RESTART (design/FABLE-SERVICE-DRAIN-RESTART-SPEC.md, work item boundary-hub-restart-robustness,
# row 1553 option A; ratification row 1557 item 2): a drained, witnessed hub handover.
# `serving/boundary_service.py`'s own startup-fixed deployment enumeration (spec §2's own
# rejection of hot-reload, row 1553 option C) means the ONLY way to add/retire a deployment is
# stop-then-start -- today that is a hand-timed blip with no witness of how long it lasted, and
# no confirmation the new process is actually healthy before the operator walks away. `restart`
# makes the interval BOUNDED (a drain timeout, never an unbounded wait), DRAINED (SIGTERM, never
# a bare kill, so in-flight requests complete), and WITNESSED (a probed health report plus a
# measured one-line summary) -- never touching `serving/boundary_service.py` itself (spec §2).
_DEFAULT_DRAIN_TIMEOUT_S = 30.0  # spec §1 leg 3's own stated default; --drain-timeout overrides.


def _parse_restart_args(argv: list[str]) -> tuple[float, bool]:
    """Hand-rolled (this file carries no argparse dependency elsewhere -- ADR-0012 P1, matching
    this module's existing style rather than introducing a second parsing convention for one
    verb). Returns (drain_timeout_s, force_kill). Raises ValueError, already formatted as
    teaching text, on anything unrecognized -- the caller turns that into a REFUSED exit 2."""
    drain_timeout = _DEFAULT_DRAIN_TIMEOUT_S
    force_kill = False
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        tok = argv[i]
        if tok == "--drain-timeout":
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                raise ValueError("--drain-timeout requires a value (seconds)")
            try:
                drain_timeout = float(argv[i + 1])
            except ValueError as e:
                raise ValueError(f"--drain-timeout value {argv[i + 1]!r} is not a number") from e
            if drain_timeout <= 0:
                raise ValueError(f"--drain-timeout must be > 0, got {drain_timeout!r}")
            i += 2
            continue
        if tok == "--force-kill":
            force_kill = True
            i += 1
            continue
        raise ValueError(
            f"unrecognized restart argument {tok!r} (known: --drain-timeout <seconds>, "
            f"--force-kill)")
    return drain_timeout, force_kill


def cmd_restart(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    try:
        drain_timeout, force_kill = _parse_restart_args(argv)
    except ValueError as e:
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- {e}. Nothing was touched.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    dep = _load_deployment()
    url, port = _boundary_url_and_port(dep)
    boundary_deployment = _boundary_deployment(dep)
    world_dir = DEPLOYMENT_PATH.parent
    toml_path = world_dir / "boundary-multiplex.toml"
    pidfile = _pidfile_path()

    # LEG 1 (spec §1.1): own-process check, verbatim posture to `stop` -- an adopted or
    # hand-started hub is not ours to restart. Refuse, teach, signal nothing.
    if not pidfile.is_file():
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- no pidfile at {pidfile}. This tool has no record "
              f"of having started the service at {url} itself (an operator's own manual start, "
              f"or a different tool) -- restarting a process this tool never started is refused "
              f"rather than guessed. Stop it where it was started, or run 'autoharn service "
              f"start' to start fresh here. Nothing was signaled.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    try:
        old_pid = int(pidfile.read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip())
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- {pidfile} does not contain a readable pid "
              f"({e.__class__.__name__}: {e}). Nothing was signaled.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if not er.pid_is_boundary_service(old_pid, toml_path):
        # Verbatim posture to `stop` (spec §1.1) means the FULL behavior, not just the refusal
        # text: `stop` removes a pidfile that fails this identity check (it no longer describes
        # anything this tool should trust, PID REUSE) rather than leaving a provably-wrong pid on
        # disk for the next invocation to trip over -- same here.
        pidfile.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- pidfile named pid {old_pid}, which is not a "
              f"serving.boundary_service process for this world's own boundary-multiplex.toml "
              f"({toml_path}) -- PID REUSE can make a stale pidfile name an unrelated process; "
              f"restarting it on trust risks signaling a bystander. The stale pidfile was "
              f"removed; nothing was signaled. Stop it where it was started, or run 'autoharn "
              f"service start' to start fresh here.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    # LEG 2 (spec §1.2): config pre-validation BEFORE any signal -- an invalid config refuses
    # HERE, with the old process untouched and still serving; a restart must never trade a
    # working hub for a config-parse crash loop.
    try:
        records = bmc.load_multiplex_config(toml_path)
    except bmc.MultiplexConfigError as e:
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- {toml_path} does not validate ({e}). The old "
              f"process (pid {old_pid}) was NOT signaled and keeps serving unchanged. Fix the "
              f"config and run 'autoharn service restart' again.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    print(f"autoharn service: config at {toml_path} validates -- the new process will serve "
          f"{sorted(records.keys())}.")

    # LEG 3 (spec §1.3): drain -- SIGTERM the old process (uvicorn's own graceful path: listen
    # socket closes, in-flight requests complete), wait bounded. On timeout, REFUSE loudly with
    # the pid still running -- never escalate to SIGKILL unasked; --force-kill is the operator's
    # explicit, printed-as-such escalation (a stricter default than `stop`'s own auto-escalate,
    # deliberate: `restart`'s whole contract is "bounded", never a silent unbounded hang, but
    # also never an unasked kill of a process that may still be finishing real work).
    t_sigterm = time.monotonic()
    try:
        os.kill(old_pid, signal.SIGTERM)
    except ProcessLookupError:
        print(f"autoharn service: pid {old_pid} (from {pidfile}) was already gone before SIGTERM "
              f"-- proceeding to spawn.", file=sys.stderr)
    except OSError as e:
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- could not signal pid {old_pid} "
              f"({e.__class__.__name__}: {e}). Nothing else was touched.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    else:
        if not _wait_for_pid_exit(old_pid, drain_timeout, _STOP_POLL_INTERVAL_S):
            if not force_kill:
                print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- pid {old_pid} did NOT exit within "
                      f"{drain_timeout}s of SIGTERM -- it is STILL RUNNING and still serving "
                      f"{url}. Never escalating to SIGKILL unasked; re-run with --force-kill to "
                      f"explicitly authorize that escalation, or investigate pid {old_pid} by "
                      f"hand (a slow in-flight request, or a hung shutdown handler).",
                      file=sys.stderr)
                return 1
            print(f"autoharn service: pid {old_pid} did NOT exit within {drain_timeout}s of "
                  f"SIGTERM -- --force-kill was passed, ESCALATING to SIGKILL.", file=sys.stderr)
            try:
                os.kill(old_pid, signal.SIGKILL)
            except ProcessLookupError:
                pass
            except OSError as e:
                print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- SIGKILL could not be sent to pid {old_pid} "
                      f"({e.__class__.__name__}: {e}). pid {old_pid} may still be alive.",
                      file=sys.stderr)
                return 1
            if not _wait_for_pid_exit(old_pid, _STOP_SIGKILL_TIMEOUT_S, _STOP_POLL_INTERVAL_S):
                print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- pid {old_pid} is STILL ALIVE "
                      f"{_STOP_SIGKILL_TIMEOUT_S}s after SIGKILL (likely stuck in "
                      f"uninterruptible kernel sleep). Investigate pid {old_pid} by hand.",
                      file=sys.stderr)
                return 1
    drain_elapsed = time.monotonic() - t_sigterm
    print(f"autoharn service: drained pid {old_pid} in {drain_elapsed:.1f}s.", file=sys.stderr)

    # LEG 4 (spec §1.4): spawn -- the existing start path verbatim (bind-as-lock, child writes
    # the pidfile after its own synchronous bind). `spawn_and_wait` re-probes and clears the now-
    # stale pidfile itself (ADR-0012 P1: reuse ensure_running's structural resolution, no new
    # race logic here).
    result = er.spawn_and_wait(world_dir, url, port, boundary_deployment)

    # LEG 5 (spec §1.5): handover deviation from `start`'s adopt-is-success, stated and
    # deliberate -- if the bind race is lost to a FOREIGN process in the gap, `restart` REFUSES
    # loudly naming the squatter instead of adopting (row 1165's adopt ruling governs `start`;
    # `restart`'s contract is stricter -- the operator's intent was "my new config is now
    # serving", and an adopted stranger structurally defeats that even if it answers /health).
    if result.status == "adopted":
        who = f"pid {result.winner_pid}" if result.winner_pid is not None else "an unidentified process"
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- pid {old_pid} was drained, but {who} won the bind "
              f"on {url} before this invocation's own spawn (pid {result.proc_pid}) could -- "
              f"this looks like a foreign process racing the freed port, not this restart's own "
              f"new config taking over. Refusing to report success over a squatter (restart's "
              f"contract is stricter than 'start': adopting here would silently defeat the "
              f"'my edited config is now serving' intent even though {who} answers its own "
              f"/health). The hub is now served by {who}, NOT by the config this invocation "
              f"just validated -- investigate immediately.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if result.status == "failed":
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- pid {old_pid} was drained, but the new process "
              f"never came up: {result.detail}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    new_pid = result.proc_pid
    t_reachable = time.monotonic()
    # "unserved window" (spec §1.7) is measured from the SIGTERM send (the earliest instant the
    # old process could plausibly have stopped accepting new connections -- uvicorn's own
    # graceful path stops accepting near-instantly on SIGTERM, well before the process fully
    # exits) through the new process's own first successful health probe inside spawn_and_wait's
    # poll loop -- the honest upper bound on "how long could a new request have found nobody
    # home", not merely the drain-wait or spawn-wait halves counted separately.
    unserved_window = t_reachable - t_sigterm

    # LEG 6 (spec §1.6): witness before reporting -- probe /d/{name}/health for EVERY deployment
    # in the newly loaded config, version-compatibly (er.probe, never bare 200-counting -- the
    # SEVERE-4 lesson). Report per deployment; exit nonzero if any fails, hub left running
    # (partially-healthy is reported, not rolled back).
    healthy: list[str] = []
    unhealthy: list[str] = []
    for name in sorted(records):
        outcome, detail = er.probe(url, name)
        if outcome == er.OK:
            healthy.append(name)
            print(f"autoharn service: SERVING {name}")
        else:
            unhealthy.append(name)
            print(f"autoharn service: {name}: {detail or outcome}", file=sys.stderr)
    if unhealthy:
        print(f"autoharn service: {len(unhealthy)}/{len(records)} deployment(s) unhealthy after "
              f"restart -- the new hub (pid {new_pid}) is left RUNNING, not rolled back (rollback "
              f"is the operator re-editing the config and running 'autoharn service restart' "
              f"again).", file=sys.stderr)

    # LEG 7 (spec §1.7): one-line summary with the measured gap.
    print(f"autoharn service: restart: drained {old_pid} in {drain_elapsed:.1f}s, new pid "
          f"{new_pid}, unserved window ~{unserved_window:.1f}s, {len(healthy)}/{len(records)} "
          f"deployments healthy.")
    return 0 if not unhealthy else 1


_SUBCOMMANDS = {"status": cmd_status, "start": cmd_start, "stop": cmd_stop, "restart": cmd_restart}


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    # Fixture-sandbox gate: strict two-token exemption, matching the shape used at every other
    # check site (the dispatcher's own `_fixture_sandbox_gate` in ./autoharn, and every
    # libexec/autoharn/<verb> preamble) -- `[ "$#" -eq 1 ] && argv[0] in ("--help", "-h")`. The
    # loose `argv[0] in (...)` test this replaced ignored trailing tokens (`--help start` would
    # exempt itself) and, worse, returned BEFORE fixture_sandbox.check() ran at all, so the
    # sandbox check never even saw the invocation. Bare argv (no args at all) is NOT this strict
    # shape either -- it does not match len(argv) == 1 -- so it is not exempt: under the marker it
    # goes through check() same as any other non-help invocation, which mirrors what
    # `./autoharn service` (bare, no args) does through the dispatcher's own gate (its exemption
    # is exactly as strict, and 0 args also fails "$#" -eq 1 there, so the marker check runs
    # before autoharn-service is even exec'd). The two entry paths -- dispatcher-mediated and this
    # file invoked directly -- now agree: bare argv under the marker is refused by the sandbox
    # check at both, not silently allowed through only when this file is invoked directly.
    strict_help = len(argv) == 1 and argv[0] in ("--help", "-h")
    if not strict_help:
        fixture_sandbox.check("autoharn service " + " ".join(argv))
    if not argv or argv[0] in ("--help", "-h"):
        # NO-MARKER help decision is intentionally unchanged from before this fix (loose
        # argv[0]-based test, so `--help start` still prints help under no marker) -- the hazard
        # fixed above is the sandbox check being skipped, not this file's own argument parsing.
        # Byte-identical NO-MARKER behavior is witnessed in
        # seen-red/fixture-sandbox-runtime-foreclosure/run_fixtures.py.
        print("usage: autoharn service status|start|stop|restart [--drain-timeout <seconds>] "
              "[--force-kill]\n\n"
              "  status   probe the boundary this world's deployment.json names; never writes\n"
              "  start    ensure-running: spawn a detached child if unreachable, or adopt\n"
              "           whichever process wins the bind race; a no-op if already reachable\n"
              "  stop     explicit stop -- only for a service THIS tool's own 'start' spawned\n"
              "           (tracked via a pidfile, and re-checked against /proc at stop time);\n"
              "           refuses, never guesses, otherwise\n"
              "  restart  drained, witnessed hub handover (design/FABLE-SERVICE-DRAIN-RESTART-\n"
              "           SPEC.md): validates the config, SIGTERMs the old process and waits\n"
              "           (--drain-timeout, default 30s; --force-kill authorizes SIGKILL only\n"
              "           after that wait is exceeded, never unasked), spawns the new process,\n"
              "           REFUSES (does not adopt) if a foreign process wins the freed port,\n"
              "           then probes every deployment's own /health before reporting the\n"
              "           measured unserved window")
        return 0
    verb = argv[0]
    if verb not in _SUBCOMMANDS:
        print(f"autoharn service: REFUSED -- unrecognized subcommand '{verb}' (known: "
              f"status, start, stop). Nothing was touched.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    return _SUBCOMMANDS[verb](argv[1:])


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
